      <li><a href="#use-cases">When each route makes sense</a></li>
      <li><a href="#brief">What to include in a sourcing brief</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq">Buyer FAQ</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="definitions">White label and private label: start with the actual scope</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>White label</strong> usually begins with an existing manufacturer-specified product. The buyer chooses from available styles and may add brand presentation such as a logo or label where the supplier supports it. The underlying style can also be supplied to other buyers, so it is generally a speed-focused, non-exclusive route.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Private label</strong> means the finished product is presented under the buyer’s brand. On activewear projects, this may start with a proven stock style and add branded logos, labels, hang tags or packaging. It can also include selected colors or details, depending on the supplier’s development capability. It should not be confused with a guarantee that the base pattern or fabric is exclusive.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Custom OEM/ODM</strong> is a different level of control. It is appropriate when the brand needs a defined fit, fabric, construction, panel layout, color program or product concept that is not simply an existing catalog style. This route needs a clearer technical brief and more sample approval discipline.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comparison">White label vs private label activewear comparison</h2>
  <div class="article-table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Decision area</th><th>White label</th><th>Private label</th><th>Custom OEM/ODM</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Base product</strong></td><td>Existing supplier-specified style</td><td>Usually an existing style selected for your brand presentation</td><td>Brand-specific product brief or development direction</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Launch speed</strong></td><td>Often the fastest route once stock details are confirmed</td><td>Depends on branding and selected modifications</td><td>Depends on development, materials and sample approvals</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Brand expression</strong></td><td>Usually limited to supported applications</td><td>Logo, labels, hang tags, packaging and selected options</td><td>Broadest control across product and presentation</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Exclusivity</strong></td><td>Normally limited</td><td>Depends on the actual agreed product scope</td><td>Can support more differentiation when the specification is unique</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Buyer preparation</strong></td><td>Catalog choice, quantity, colors, sizes and branding request</td><td>Clear brand files and an approval brief for each selected style</td><td>Tech pack or detailed product direction, sample feedback and approvals</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="use-cases">When should a brand use each model?</h2>
  <h3>Use white label activewear to validate a commercial idea</h3>
  <p>White label is helpful when speed matters more than product exclusivity: a first ecommerce launch, a studio merchandise program, an event capsule or a retailer testing a new activewear category. Keep the assortment focused and confirm the current stock, colors and size mix rather than assuming every catalog option is available.</p>
  <h3>Use private label activewear to make a selected range feel like your brand</h3>
  <p>Private label is the stronger route when the buyer needs more than a generic resale product. A clear logo treatment, correct care label, hang tag and packaging can make a matching yoga set or legging collection feel coherent. The buyer should still check whether the fabric, fit and color direction match the intended customer before adding branding.</p>
  <h3>Use custom OEM/ODM when product control is the commercial advantage</h3>
  <p>If the goal is an original sports bra support structure, unique legging fit, a special fabric hand feel or an exclusive color and panel direction, use custom development. This is not automatically the right first step; it is the right step when the expected product differentiation justifies the extra specification and sample work.</p>

  <figure class="article-image article-image-wide">
    <img src="/images/products/shared/ready-stock-warehouse.webp" alt="Fitclo ready-stock activewear warehouse with packed garments and shelving" width="750" height="1000" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>Ready-stock programs need current confirmation of the selected style, color, size mix and branding route before the buyer plans a launch.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="brief">The buyer brief that creates a useful quote</h2>
  <p>A vague request for “private label workout clothes” creates a vague response. Whether you choose white label, private label or custom development, start with the same core information.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Product direction:</strong> leggings, sports bras, yoga sets, gym shorts, jackets or a defined capsule.</li>
    <li><strong>Business goal:</strong> quick launch, market test, branded stock program or differentiated long-term collection.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantity and size plan:</strong> estimated order quantity plus the style-color-size mix.</li>
    <li><strong>Visual direction:</strong> reference images, preferred colors and your expected retail position.</li>
    <li><strong>Branding requirements:</strong> logo artwork, placement, labels, hang tags and packaging needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Approval plan:</strong> which sample, fabric, fit and finished-branding details must be approved before bulk production.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>FITCLO has supported global activewear buyers since 2010. The best route is the one that makes the product, brand presentation and reorder plan clear before the first bulk order—not simply the route with the quickest label application.</p>

